#: ../../../user/advanced-topics/salt.rst:9 1fe90ce9da0841439ccc2303acf8c83a
msgid ""
"Since the Qubes R3.1 release we have included the Salt (also called "
"SaltStack) management engine in dom0 as default (with some states already "
"configured). Salt allows administrators to easily configure their systems. "
"In this guide we will show how it is set up and how you can modify it for "
"your own purpose."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../user/advanced-topics/salt.rst:11 6f983cb7b812402dad5af5ceae133a89
msgid ""
"In the current form the **API is provisional** and subject to change between "
"*minor* releases."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../user/advanced-topics/salt.rst:14 9e6e6c94a90f4a5f8eb7606005011102
msgid "Understanding Salt"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../user/advanced-topics/salt.rst:17 71ae7f00d42144ea8493d508cb20f1de
msgid ""
"This document is not meant to be comprehensive Salt documentation; however, "
"before writing anything it is required you have at least *some* "
"understanding of basic Salt-related vocabulary. For more exhaustive "
"documentation, visit `official site <https://docs.saltproject.io/en/latest/"
">`__, though we must warn you that it is not easy to read if you just start "
"working with Salt and know nothing."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../user/advanced-topics/salt.rst:20 e0e60bae9016476b9876c6979935b95d
msgid "The Salt Architecture"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../user/advanced-topics/salt.rst:23 9396cf48c1c34763b8c9789d6cadeade
msgid ""
"Salt is a client-server model, where the server (called *master*) manages "
"its clients (called *minions*). In typical situations, it is intended that "
"the administrator interacts only with the master and keeps the "
"configurations there. In Qubes, we don’t have a master. Instead we have one "
"minion which resides in ``dom0`` and manages qubes from there. This setup is "
"also supported by Salt."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../user/advanced-topics/salt.rst:25 abd952a7a8f741dbb5d322c2b5f60ec5
msgid ""
"Salt is a management engine (similar to Ansible, Puppet, and Chef), that "
"enforces a particular state of a minion system. A *state* is an end effect "
"*declaratively* expressed by the administrator. This is the most important "
"concept in the entire engine. All configurations (i.e., the states) are "
"written in YAML."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../user/advanced-topics/salt.rst:27 49952ea0fa0a4a4b8d0fa3da1c07f1e0
msgid ""
"A *pillar* is a data back-end declared by the administrator. When states "
"become repetitive, instead of pure YAML they can be written using a template "
"engine (preferably Jinja2); which can use data structures specified in "
"pillars."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../user/advanced-topics/salt.rst:29 cfc3ecad589443d3af2f1f1f6261f278
msgid ""
"A *formula* is a ready to use, packaged solution that combines a state and a "
"pillar (possibly with some file templates and other auxiliary files). There "
"are many formulas made by helpful people all over the Internet."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../user/advanced-topics/salt.rst:31 e1ef222352924b2d9f95eb9ce246141f
msgid ""
"A *grain* is some data that is also available in templates, but its value is "
"not directly specified by administrator. For example, the distribution (e."
"g., ``\"Debian\"`` or ``\"Gentoo\"``) is a value of the grain ``\"os\"``. It "
"also contains other information about the kernel, hardware, etc."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../user/advanced-topics/salt.rst:33 5735aff8a8964280aad70bbd901746eb
msgid ""
"A *module* is a Python extension to Salt that is responsible for actually "
"enforcing the state in a particular area. It exposes some *imperative* "
"functions for the administrator. For example, there is a ``system`` module "
"that has a ``system.halt`` function that, when issued, will immediately halt "
"a domain."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../user/advanced-topics/salt.rst:35 d70bd0d3e0554f62b560f6e4cfc18d2c
msgid ""
"In salt, there are different levels of functionality. The lowest level is a "
"single state function, called like this ``state.single pkg.installed "
"name=firefox-esr`` When the system compiles data from sls formulas, it "
"generates *chunks* - low chunks are at the bottom of the compiler . You can "
"call them with ``state.low``. Next up is the *lowstate* level - this is the "
"list of all low chunks in order. To see them you have ``state."
"show_lowstate``, and use ``state.lowstate`` to apply them. At the top level "
"is *highstate* - this is an interpretation of **all** the data represented "
"in YAML in sls files. You can view it with ``state.show_highstate``."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../user/advanced-topics/salt.rst:37 2df530dfcc7849ef83d5fb33e8c70f12
msgid ""
"When you want to apply a configuration, you can use ``qubesctl state."
"highstate.`` This will apply all the states you have included in highstate."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../user/advanced-topics/salt.rst:39 770887723ba04e4da684745b8c80fd44
msgid ""
"There is another function, ``state.apply``; ``state.apply`` has two uses. "
"When used on its own, it will apply *highstate* - all the configuration that "
"has been enabled. It can also be used to apply a specific state, like this: "
"``state.apply browser`` - this will apply the state specified in ``browser."
"sls``."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../user/advanced-topics/salt.rst:41 68eb9c7e0e2346b3b473e03fd3d4ba8a
msgid ""
"For simplicity we will use ``state.apply`` through this page, when we want "
"to apply all configured states."
msgstr ""

